{
  "image_name": "2c92d0a0fd627825f6073b75d07e6b62.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a microfabrication workflow for building a PDMS microfluidic device and then integrating it with a porous membrane for a blood–air interface model. It begins with a silicon wafer coated with SU-8 photoresist. A photomask is aligned over the resist so that light exposure creates a polymerized channel feature, and development leaves a raised mold pattern on the wafer. PDMS is then cast over this mold and peeled off, producing a transparent slab containing the negative of the channel network.\n\nIn the final assembly, this patterned PDMS layer is combined with another PDMS layer and a perforated membrane positioned between them. The lower region appears to form a blood channel, while the upper region serves as the air side. A magnified cross-section suggests that cells line the membrane on the air-facing surface, creating a barrier above the blood compartment. Overall, the figure shows how standard soft lithography is used to fabricate a layered organ-on-chip style device in which fluidic channels and a porous membrane recreate a separated but interacting air and blood interface.",
